            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dysprosium"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/element/66")],
            element_info: Some((
                atomic_number: 66,
                atomic_weight: 162.5,
                group: None,
                period: Some(6),
                block: Some("f"),
                electron_config: Some("[Xe] 4f10 6s2"),
                electronegativity_pauling: Some(1.22),
                first_ionization_kj_mol: Some(573.030300585),
                electron_affinity_kj_mol: Some(33.962720000000004),
                atomic_radius_pm: Some(175.0),
                discoverer: Some("Paul Émile Lecoq de Boisbaudran"),
                discovery_year: Some(1886),
                overview: Some("Dysprosium is a chemical element; it has symbol Dy and atomic number 66. It is a rare-earth element in the lanthanide series with a metallic silver luster. Dysprosium is never found in nature as a free element, though, like other lanthanides, it is found in various minerals, such as xenotime. Naturally occurring dysprosium is composed of seven isotopes, the most abundant of which is 164Dy.

Dysprosium was first identified in 1886 by Paul Émile Lecoq de Boisbaudran, but it was not isolated in pure form until the development of ion-exchange techniques in the 1950s. Dysprosium is used to produce neodymium-iron-boron (NdFeB) magnets, which are crucial for electric vehicle motors and the efficient operation of wind turbines. It is used for its high thermal neutron absorption cross-section in making control rods in nuclear reactors, for its high magnetic susceptibility (χv ≈ 5.44×10−3) in data-storage applications, and as a component of Terfenol-D (a magnetostrictive material). Soluble dysprosium salts are mildly toxic, while the insoluble salts are considered non-toxic."),
                discovery_story: None,
                isotopes_summary: Some("39 known isotopes; stable: 164Dy (28.3%), 162Dy (25.5%), 163Dy (24.9%), 161Dy (18.9%); 27 radioactive"),
                magnetic_ordering: None,
            )),
        ),
