            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bismuth"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/element/83")],
            element_info: Some((
                atomic_number: 83,
                atomic_weight: 208.9804,
                group: Some(15),
                period: Some(6),
                block: Some("p"),
                electron_config: Some("[Xe] 4f14 5d10 6s2 6p3"),
                electronegativity_pauling: Some(1.9),
                first_ionization_kj_mol: Some(702.94301126),
                electron_affinity_kj_mol: Some(90.92379757),
                atomic_radius_pm: Some(160.0),
                discoverer: Some("Known to the ancients."),
                discovery_year: None,
                overview: Some("Bismuth is a chemical element; it has symbol Bi and atomic number 83. It is a post-transition metal and one of the pnictogens, with chemical properties resembling its lighter group 15 siblings arsenic and antimony. Elemental bismuth occurs naturally, and its sulfide and oxide forms are important commercial ores. The free element is 86% as dense as lead. It is a brittle metal with a silvery-white color when freshly produced. Surface oxidation generally gives samples of the metal a somewhat rosy cast. Further oxidation under heat can give bismuth a vividly iridescent appearance due to thin-film interference. Bismuth is both the most diamagnetic element and one of the least thermally conductive metals known.

Bismuth was formerly understood to be the element with the highest atomic mass whose nuclei do not spontaneously decay. However, in 2003 it was found to be very slightly radioactive. The metal's only primordial isotope, bismuth-209, undergoes alpha decay with a half-life roughly a billion times longer than the estimated age of the universe."),
                discovery_story: None,
                isotopes_summary: Some("41 known isotopes; stable: 185Bi, 221Bi, 222Bi, 223Bi; 36 radioactive"),
                magnetic_ordering: None,
            )),
        ),
    1084: (
