            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [IsAcid],
            description: Some("Valeric acid or  pentanoic acid is a straight-chain alkyl carboxylic acid with the chemical formula CH3(CH2)3COOH. Like other low-molecular-weight carboxylic acids, it has an unpleasant odor.  It is found in the perennial flowering plant Valeriana officinalis, from which it gets its name. Its primary use is in the synthesis of its esters. Salts and esters of valeric acid are known as valerates or pentanoates. Volatile esters of valeric acid tend to have pleasant odors and are used in perfumes and cosmetics. Several, including ethyl valerate and pentyl valerate are used as food additives because of their fruity flavors."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: Some("H314: Causes severe skin burns and eye damage [Danger Skin corrosion/irritation]"),
            uses: ["Intermediates", "All Other Basic Organic Chemical Manufacturing", "As sugarcane ripening agent", "Intermediate"],
            logp: Some(1.261),
            appearance: Some("Pentanoic acid appears as a colorless liquid with a penetrating unpleasant odor. Density 0.94 g / cm3. Freezing point -93.2 °F (-34 °C). Boiling point 365.7 °F (185.4 °C). Flash point 192 °F (88.9 °C). Corrosive to metals and tissue."),
            synonyms: ["Valerate", "n-BuCOOH", "N-C4H9COOH", "Pentanoate", "Valeric acid", "n-Pentanoate", "pentoic acid", "N-Valeric Acid"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=1393189"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/7991"), ("ChEBI", "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI_17418"), ("Wikidata", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q407796"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C109524&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
