            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [IsAcid, IsBase],
            description: Some("Anthranilic acid is an aromatic acid with the formula C6H4(NH2)(CO2H) and has a sweetish taste. The molecule consists of a benzene ring, ortho-substituted with a carboxylic acid and an amine. As a result of containing both acidic and basic functional groups, the compound is amphoteric. Anthranilic acid is a white solid when pure, although commercial samples may appear yellow. The anion [C6H4(NH2)(CO2)]−, obtained by the deprotonation of anthranilic acid, is called anthranilate. Anthranilic acid was once thought to be a vitamin and was referred to as vitamin L1 in that context, but it is now known to be non-essential in human nutrition."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: Some("H318 (49.5%): Causes serious eye damage [Danger Serious eye damage/eye irritation]"),
            uses: ["All Other Basic Organic Chemical Manufacturing", "Dyes", "Dyes, drugs, perfumes, and pharmaceuticals."],
            logp: Some(0.967),
            appearance: Some("Anthranilic acid appears as odorless white to pale-yellow or tan crystalline powder with a sweetish taste. (NTP, 1992)"),
            synonyms: ["vitamin L", "H-2-Abz-OH", "Vitamin L1", "anthranic acid", "carboxyaniline", "2-carboxyaniline", "o-carboxyaniline", "o-anthranilic acid"],
