            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [],
            description: Some("1,4-Dioxane () is a heterocyclic organic compound, classified as an ether. It is a colorless liquid with a faint sweet odor similar to that of diethyl ether. The compound is often called simply dioxane because the other dioxane isomers (1,2- and 1,3-) are rarely encountered. 
1,4-Dioxane is miscible in water, essentially nonvolatile when dissolved in water, not well adsorbed by activated carbon, and not readily oxidized by common oxidants.
Dioxane is used as a solvent in manufacturing applications, and as a stabilizer for the transport of chlorinated hydrocarbons in aluminium containers. It is a highly flammable substance that produces toxic vapors when heated.
Although it is a trace material in commonly used products, such as cosmetics, dioxane is considered a hazardous contaminant and potential carcinogen in many countries, requiring government monitoring of amounts used in manufacturing and its presence in air, drinking water, and ecosystems."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: None,
            uses: ["Paints and Coatings", "Both", "Commercial"],
            logp: Some(0.033),
            appearance: Some("Colorless liquid"),
            synonyms: ["Dioxane", "123-91-1", "p-Dioxane", "Diethylene ether", "1,4-Diethylene dioxide", "1,4-Dioxacyclohexane", "Dioxan", "Tetrahydro-1,4-dioxin"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=1572944"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C123911&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
