            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [],
            description: Some("Maltose ( or ), also known as maltobiose or malt sugar, is a disaccharide formed from two units of glucose joined with an α(1→4) bond. In the isomer isomaltose, the two glucose molecules are joined with an α(1→6) bond. Maltose is the two-unit member of the amylose homologous series, the key structural motif of starch. When beta-amylase breaks down starch, it removes two glucose units at a time, producing maltose. An example of this reaction is found in germinating seeds, which is why it was named after malt. Unlike sucrose, it is a reducing sugar."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: None,
            uses: ["CIR ingredient: Maltose"],
            logp: Some(-5.555),
            appearance: None,
            synonyms: ["D-maltose", "Malt sugar", "Malzzucker", "maltobiose", "Cextromaltose", "D-(+)-maltose", "alpha-malt sugar", "alpha-D-Glcp-(1->4)-D-Glcp"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=212786"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/303614"), ("ChEBI", "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI_17306"), ("Wikidata", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q69758827"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C69794&Units=SI")],
