            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [],
            description: Some("Acetylene (systematic name: ethyne) is a chemical compound with the formula C2H2 and structure HC≡CH. It is a hydrocarbon and the simplest alkyne. This colorless gas is widely used as a fuel and a chemical building block. It is unstable in its pure form and thus is usually handled as a solution. Pure acetylene is odorless, but commercial grades usually have a marked odor due to impurities such as divinyl sulfide and phosphine.
As an alkyne, acetylene is unsaturated because its two carbon atoms are bonded together in a triple bond. The carbon–carbon triple bond places all four atoms in the same straight line, with CCH bond angles of 180°. The triple bond in acetylene results in a high energy content that is released when acetylene is burned."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: Some("H220: Extremely flammable gas [Danger Flammable gases]"),
            uses: ["Fuel", "Fuels and fuel additives", "Intermediates", "Soil amendments (fertilizers)", "Construction"],
            logp: Some(0.249),
            appearance: None,
            synonyms: ["C2H2", "HCCH", "CH#CH", "Ethin", "HC#CH", "Ethyne", "ethine", "Acetylen"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=1778"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/6326"), ("ChEBI", "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI_27518"), ("Wikidata", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q133145"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C74862&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
