            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [],
            description: Some("Iodomethane, also called methyl iodide, and commonly abbreviated \"MeI\", is the chemical compound with the formula CH3I.  It is a dense, colorless, volatile liquid.  In terms of chemical structure, it is related to methane by replacement of one hydrogen atom by an atom of iodine. It is naturally emitted in small amounts by rice plantations. It is also produced in vast quantities estimated to be greater than 214,000 tons annually by algae and kelp in the world's temperate oceans, and in lesser amounts on land by terrestrial fungi and bacteria. It is used in organic synthesis as a source of methyl groups."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: Some("H301: Toxic if swallowed [Danger Acute toxicity, oral]"),
            uses: ["All Other Basic Organic Chemical Manufacturing", "Catalyst", "Pharmaceutical and Medicine Manufacturing"],
            logp: None,
            appearance: Some("Methyl iodide appears as a colorless liquid that turns brown on exposure to light. Denser than water. Contact may irritate skin, eyes and mucous membranes. Very toxic by ingestion, inhalation and skin absorption."),
            synonyms: ["MeI", "CH3I", "Halon 10001", "iodomethane", "monoiodomethane", "pralidoxime iodide", "pralidoximi iodidum", "iodure de pralidoxime"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=2250747"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/6328"), ("ChEBI", "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI_32038"), ("Wikidata", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q421729"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C74884&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
    7834: (
            id: 7834,
            name: "Methylamine",
            formula: "CH3NH2",
            state: Gas,
            molar_mass_g_mol: 31.057,
            color_hex: None,
            melting_point_c: Some(-93.1),
            boiling_point_c: Some(-6.35),
            density_g_cm3: Some(0.6562),
            heat_capacity_solid_j_g_c: None,
            heat_capacity_liquid_j_g_c: None,
            heat_capacity_gas_j_g_c: Some(1.0465),
            enthalpy_fusion_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_vaporization_kj_mol: None,
            enthalpy_formation_kj_mol: Some(18.77),
            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [IsBase],
            description: Some("Methylamine, also known as methanamine, is an organic compound with a formula of CH3NH2. This colorless gas is a derivative of ammonia, but with one hydrogen atom being replaced by a methyl group. It is the simplest primary amine.
Methylamine is sold as a solution in methanol, ethanol, tetrahydrofuran, or water, or as the anhydrous gas in pressurized metal containers. Industrially, methylamine is transported in its anhydrous form in pressurized railcars and tank trailers. It has a strong odor similar to rotten fish. Methylamine is used as a building block for the synthesis of numerous other commercially available compounds."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: Some("H220: Extremely flammable gas [Danger Flammable gases]"),
            uses: ["All Other Basic Organic Chemical Manufacturing", "Dyes", "Intermediate", "Intermediates"],
            logp: None,
            appearance: Some("Methylamine, aqueous solution appears as a colorless to yellow aqueous solution of a gas. Odor ranges from fishlike to ammonia-like as the vapor concentration increases. Flash point (of 30% solution) 34 °F. Corrosive to skin and eyes. Less dense than water; vapors heavier than air. Produces toxic oxides of nitrogen during combustion."),
            synonyms: ["MMA", "NMA", "NME", "MeNH2", "CH3-NH2", "Methanamine", "carbinamine", "aminomethane"],
