            solubility_g_per_100ml_h2o: None,
            standard_potential_v: None,
            half_life_seconds: None,
            decay_product_id: None,
            decay_particle_id: None,
            properties: [],
            description: Some("Furfural is an organic compound with the formula C4H3OCHO. It is a colorless liquid, although commercial samples are often brown. It has an aldehyde group attached to the 2-position of furan. It is a product of the dehydration of sugars, as occurs in a variety of agricultural byproducts, including corncobs, oat, wheat bran, and sawdust. The name furfural comes from the Latin word furfur, meaning bran, referring to its usual source. Furfural is derived only from dried biomass. In addition to ethanol, acetic acid, and sugar, furfural is one of the oldest known organic chemicals available readily purified from natural precursors."),
            text_source: Some("Wikipedia"),
            hazard_summary: Some("H301: Toxic if swallowed [Danger Acute toxicity, oral]"),
            uses: ["All Other Basic Organic Chemical Manufacturing", "Constituent of rubber cements", "Hardener"],
            logp: Some(1.092),
            appearance: Some("Furfural appears as colorless or reddish-brown mobile liquids with a penetrating odor. Flash points 140 °F. Denser than water and soluble in water. Vapors heavier than air. May be toxic by ingestion, skin absorption or inhalation."),
            synonyms: ["Fural", "Furol", "Furale", "Furole", "Furfurol", "a-Furole", "FEMA 2489", "Furfurale"],
            references: [("Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/?curid=490020"), ("PubChem", "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/7362"), ("ChEBI", "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI_34768"), ("Wikidata", "http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q412429"), ("NIST", "https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C98011&Units=SI")],
            element_info: None,
        ),
